WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:02.135
What are they thinking?

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.504
Welcome to Unfiltered. I'm S.E. Cupp.

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:06.639
If you've been following the 2024

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:08.408
presidential primaries,

00:00:08.408 --> 00:00:09.309
you know that

00:00:09.309 --> 00:00:10.010
every week

00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:11.978
seemingly brings a new hopeful

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.413
into the races.

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.948
Over on the left, President Joe Biden

00:00:14.948 --> 00:00:15.982
as challengers

00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:19.052
in anti-vax crusader Robert F Kennedy Jr.

00:00:19.486 --> 00:00:21.588
Spiritual leader Marianne Williamson

00:00:21.588 --> 00:00:23.156
and most recently scholar

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.025
activist Cornel West

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:25.658
who is running

00:00:25.658 --> 00:00:27.761
on the People's Party ticket.

00:00:27.761 --> 00:00:29.896
On the right, it's getting very crowded.

00:00:29.896 --> 00:00:31.998
Former President Donald Trump,

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.334
former Vice President Mike Pence.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.369
Florida Governor Ron DeSantis.

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.437
Former New Jersey

00:00:37.437 --> 00:00:39.139
Governor Chris Christie.

00:00:39.139 --> 00:00:39.873
Former South

00:00:39.873 --> 00:00:41.975
Carolina Governor Nikki Haley.

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:42.542
And U.S.

00:00:42.542 --> 00:00:43.176
Senator Tim

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:44.677
Scott of South Carolina

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:46.579
are all announced candidates

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.281
on the Republican ticket.

00:00:48.281 --> 00:00:50.583
Those are some pretty familiar names.

00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:51.751
But have you heard

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:54.320
of these GOP contenders?

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.955
Doug Burgum,

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.724
he's the governor of North Dakota

00:00:57.724 --> 00:00:58.892
and he's running for president,

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:00.894
apparently Asa Hutchinson,

00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:03.063
he's the former governor of Arkansas.

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:04.998
And he's also running.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.233
Steve Laffey, he's the former

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.202
mayor of Cranston, Rhode Island.

00:01:09.202 --> 00:01:09.936
Population

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:13.106
80 2000 hometown of my husband.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.608
What about Michigan businessman

00:01:15.608 --> 00:01:17.377
Perry Johnson,

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.479
former Montana secretary of State?

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:21.414
Corey Stapleton

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.350
or tax consultant John Anthony Castro?

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:25.251
Yes.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:26.553
All of these people

00:01:26.553 --> 00:01:28.621
and more are running for president.

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:30.123
In 20, 24.

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:31.591
That's over a dozen declared

00:01:31.591 --> 00:01:32.425
Republican candidates

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:33.159
so far,

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:34.194
with a few others

00:01:34.194 --> 00:01:36.362
mulling additional bids.

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:37.831
It's starting to feel eerily

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.632
similar to 2016

00:01:39.632 --> 00:01:40.800
when 17 people

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.836
voted for president on the right.

00:01:42.836 --> 00:01:44.370
We all know how that went.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.038
Trump used

00:01:45.038 --> 00:01:47.340
the saturation to his advantage

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.508
and won the nomination

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.043
with just a plurality,

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:52.412
not a majority of the vote

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:54.747
this year with many early primary states

00:01:54.747 --> 00:01:55.849
winner takes all.

00:01:55.849 --> 00:01:56.950
Trump could eke out

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:57.817
narrow wins

00:01:57.817 --> 00:02:00.220
and still collect all their delegates.

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:01.321
That means that every time

00:02:01.321 --> 00:02:02.555
someone else gets in the race,

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:03.690
Trump's chances

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.258
of clinching the nomination again

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.427
get better and better.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.662
That math is explicitly why Republicans

00:02:09.662 --> 00:02:11.831
like former Maryland Governor Larry Hogan

00:02:11.831 --> 00:02:12.332
and current

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:13.299
New Hampshire Governor Chris

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:15.401
Sununu decided not to run.

00:02:15.401 --> 00:02:16.836
They don't want to help Trump

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:19.339
Presumably, some of these

00:02:19.339 --> 00:02:21.174
candidates believe they can beat Trump,

00:02:21.174 --> 00:02:23.209
who's polling at 53% among

00:02:23.376 --> 00:02:25.011
Republican registered voters.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.113
According to a May CNN poll,

00:02:27.113 --> 00:02:28.114
DeSantis is polling

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.316
closest to Trump at 26%.

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:33.753
Pence and Haley are behind him at 6%.

00:02:34.587 --> 00:02:37.223
Scott and Christie are polling at 2%,

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:39.659
Hutchinson in Burgum at 1%.

00:02:39.659 --> 00:02:40.293
Now, look, it's

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:42.929
a long 18 months until November 2024

00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:44.364
and a lot can happen.

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.265
But you have to wonder

00:02:45.265 --> 00:02:47.400
what are some of these folks thinking?

00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:49.402
Are these campaigns

00:02:49.402 --> 00:02:51.271
merely vanity projects,

00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:52.972
book sales boosters,

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:54.807
fundraising missions,

00:02:54.807 --> 00:02:55.275
a chance

00:02:55.275 --> 00:02:57.410
to see the country on donors dimes?

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.013
With the stakes so high this year

00:03:00.013 --> 00:03:02.048
and Trump poised to claw his way back

00:03:02.282 --> 00:03:04.050
into the White House, where he spent

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:04.684
four years

00:03:04.684 --> 00:03:05.985
trying to systematically

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:08.054
dismantle democracy.

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:10.857
Is this really the time to say why not?

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:12.525
And hope for a miracle?

00:03:13.593 --> 00:03:14.093
I get it.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:14.794
Everyone thinks

00:03:14.794 --> 00:03:16.396
they can save the country

00:03:16.396 --> 00:03:18.631
but there are practical matters here.

00:03:18.798 --> 00:03:20.200
Name recognition,

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:20.934
money,

00:03:20.934 --> 00:03:23.369
resources, and most importantly, math.

00:03:24.037 --> 00:03:25.471
Lamentably running for president

00:03:25.471 --> 00:03:25.972
in America

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:28.408
is not an equal opportunity endeavor

00:03:28.975 --> 00:03:30.143
for anyone of age.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.245
And citizenship can technically run,

00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:34.147
but very few are ever actually

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:36.049
in positions to win

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:36.583
the party's

00:03:36.583 --> 00:03:37.483
governing bodies

00:03:37.483 --> 00:03:39.652
ensure that that's the case.

00:03:39.652 --> 00:03:41.888
So this feels a little self-serving.

00:03:42.322 --> 00:03:44.557
And isn't that the exact quality?

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:47.160
We don't want in a president again?

00:03:48.061 --> 00:03:49.862
That's it for me. See you next week.

